<!DOCTYPE qhelp PUBLIC
  "-//Semmle//qhelp//EN"
  "qhelp.dtd">
<qhelp>
<overview>
<p>
A top-level derivation attribute looks like a typo of a known
<code>stdenv.mkDerivation</code> or Python-wrapper attribute.
</p>
<p>
This query is deliberately narrow: it only reports case-only mistakes
(such as <code>pName</code> for <code>pname</code>) and a curated set of common
transpositions and misspellings (such as <code>configFlags</code> for
<code>configureFlags</code>). Unknown custom attributes are otherwise left
alone to keep the signal high.
</p>
</overview>

<recommendation>
<p>
Rename the attribute to the suggested canonical spelling. If the name is
intentional and package-local, prefer an underscore-prefixed helper
attribute (for example <code>_helper</code>) or move package metadata under
<code>meta</code> instead of adding an accidental top-level derivation key.
</p>
</recommendation>

<example>
<p>Problematic — likely typos:</p>
<sample language="nix">
{ stdenv }:
stdenv.mkDerivation {
  pName = "demo";
  version = "1.0";
  configFlags = [ "--enable-foo" ];
}
</sample>
<p>Recommended — use the canonical attribute names:</p>
<sample language="nix">
{ stdenv }:
stdenv.mkDerivation {
  pname = "demo";
  version = "1.0";
  configureFlags = [ "--enable-foo" ];
}
</sample>
</example>

<references>
<li>
<a href="https://github.com/jtojnar/nixpkgs-hammering/blob/main/explanations/attribute-typo.md">
<code>nixpkgs-hammering</code> explanation: <code>attribute-typo</code></a>
</li>
<li>
<a href="https://nixos.org/manual/nixpkgs/stable/#sec-using-stdenv">
Nixpkgs manual: <code>stdenv.mkDerivation</code></a>
</li>
</references>
</qhelp>
